71:  * Invert the Schiller & Naumann drag correlation for the particle Reynolds number.
72:  *
73:  * A force balance on a sphere gives
74:  *
75:  *     (pi/6) * d^3 * (rho_p - rho_f) * g = Cd * (pi/4) * d^2 * rho_f * u^2 / 2
76:  *
77:  * which rearranges to Ar = (3/4) * Cd * Re^2. Note the factor is 3/4, not 4/3;
78:  * an earlier version of this comment had it inverted. The CODE was correct
79:  * throughout and the solver below was checked against the Stokes limit, so this
80:  * correction is to the documentation only. Substituting the Schiller & Naumann
81:  * drag coefficient Cd = (24/Re) * (1 + 0.15 * Re^0.687) reduces this to the
82:  * IMPLICIT equation
83:  *
84:  *     Ar = 18 * Re * (1 + 0.15 * Re^0.687)
85:  *
86:  * f(Re) = 18*Re*(1 + 0.15*Re^0.687) - Ar is strictly increasing for Re > 0, with
87:  * f(0) = -Ar < 0 and f(Ar/18) > 0. Bisection on [0, Ar/18] is therefore
88:  * guaranteed to converge ΓÇö unlike an unchecked Newton step, it cannot diverge.
89:  */
90: function solveSchillerNaumannRe(archimedesNumber: number): { re: number; iterations: number; residual: number } {
91:   let lo = 0;
92:   let hi = archimedesNumber / 18;
93:   let re = hi;
94:   let iterations = 0;
95:   let residual = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
96:   for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
97:     iterations = i + 1;
98:     re = (lo + hi) / 2;
99:     residual = 18 * re * (1 + 0.15 * Math.pow(re, 0.687)) - archimedesNumber;
100:     if (Math.abs(residual) <= 1e-10 * archimedesNumber) break;
101:     if (residual < 0) lo = re;
102:     else hi = re;
103:   }
104:   return { re, iterations, residual };
105: }
