// Y at which the downcomer stops descending and the inlet elbow begins.
  //
  // ITEM 31 ΓÇö this must sit one bend radius BELOW the inlet centreline, not 0.35 m
  // below it.
  //
  // The exit was `max(inletYM + 0.35, 3.2)`, so the elbow arrived at the tangential
  // inlet with only 350 mm of rise to find. A 90┬░ long-radius elbow needs its own
  // radius in each direction, so there was never enough vertical room: the drawn
  // bend had to climb while it was still turning, which is precisely the "never
  // smooth, always bent" symptom.
  //
  // It also made the length accounting wrong. The transition was costed as a fixed
  // elbow plus spool while the drawn pipe included a 0.35 m climb that nothing
  // counted, so the centreline came out 5-15% long and the assembly length gate
  // failed on every capacity case.
  //
  // Giving the elbow exactly one radius of rise makes it tangent-continuous by
  // construction: the pipe leaves the downcomer vertical, sweeps a quarter circle
  // of the engine's own bend radius, and arrives at the inlet travelling purely
  // horizontal, which is what a tangential inlet requires.
  const downcomerExitY = Math.max(cycloneInletYM + bendRadiusM, 3.2);

